 excluding trace while segment 1 takes around 600ms, but I need the exact per-segment breakdown to be sure.\n\nNow I'm considering a new optimization approach: multi-stream Merkle tree construction during trace commit. Currently the Merkle tree building calls a single GPU kernel to hash all rows, then sequentially processes each tree layer. With the `k_whir` parameter, rows are grouped together, and the kernel uses block dimensions to compute multiple rows per thread. The idea would be to parallelize these layer computations across multiple GPU streams instead of processing them sequentially.\n\nBut looking at the actual implementation, the bottom layer is already a single monolithic kernel that's heavily parallelized across all positions, so multi-streaming wouldn't provide any benefit there. The compress layers are also each single kernels that depend on the previous layer's output, making them inherently sequential. So this approach wouldn't actually help.\n\nLet me pivot to a different idea: overlapping the trace commit phase with the constraints phase startup.\n\nCurrently the prover commits the trace, observes the commitment in the transcript to get challenges, then runs the constraints phase. The problem is that steps 2 and 3 both depend on step 1 — we can't compute challenges or set up the constraints until we have the commitment hash. And that hash is the final output of the Merkle tree construction, so there's no room to parallelize there either.\n\nI'm wondering if we could start some preparation work while the lower Merkle layers are being built, but that runs into the same issue: the metadata structures we'd want to precompute depend on the challenges, which depend on the root. It's a circular dependency baked into the protocol itself.\n\nLet me explore a different angle — what if we reduced the width of the RS code matrix? The matrix width is currently the sum of all trace columns, and at APC 300 that's substantial. The NTT and Merkle tree both scale with this width, so shrinking it would proportionally reduce their work. We could potentially pre-hash groups of columns into a smaller representation, but that would fundamentally change the commitment scheme.\n\nActually, looking at how the code is structured, there's only one stacked commitment per segment across all traces, so there's no parallelism opportunity there either.\n\nLet me step back and look at the actual performance bottlenecks more systematically.\n\nExamining the baseline metrics... Looking at the performance breakdown, MLE Rounds has the smallest improvement ratio at 1.13x despite being relatively fast, while LogUp GKR remains the biggest bottleneck even after optimization. The LogUp GKR phase breaks down into two main parts: evaluating the interaction expressions across all AIRs to generate the GKR input, which takes the bulk of the time around 300-320ms, and then the fractional sumcheck protocol itself.\n\nNow I'm examining the fractional sumcheck kernels in detail to see if there's an unexplored optimization opportunity there. The profiling data shows several kernels contributing to this phase—compute_round_and_revert_kernel is the heaviest at 70ms across many instances, followed by bit reversal and multifold operations, with various tree-building and folding kernels making up the rest.\n\nWhat's interesting is that the total GPU kernel time adds up to around 241ms, but the actual wall-clock measurement for the GKR protocol is only 200-210ms. This suggests there's CPU/GPU overlap happening, or the kernels are running concurrently in ways that reduce the critical path. The main bottleneck appears to be the compute_round_and_revert_kernel, which handles polynomial evaluation and reversal operations.\n\nLooking at the other major kernels—bit reversal for building the fractional tree levels, the multifold operations that compress multiple rounds, and the precomputation of partial products—they're all working with large arrays of fractional elements. Given that we're processing 448 million bus interaction messages across a tree depth of log₂(67,357) rounds, the question is whether any of these core operations have optimization headroom. The structure is fairly rigid since each round must fold the tree by exactly one level, but there might be opportunities in how the fractional arithmetic itself is computed or cached. The per-call time of roughly 8ms for the bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel is meaningful, especially when multiplied across segments. I'm stepping back from the deep GKR protocol analysis since I don't have enough context to propose a solid optimization. Instead, I'm thinking about this from an ROI angle—what could realistically save over 40ms at APC 300, has precedent in this codebase, isn't retreading failed approaches, and targets the worst-scaling bottlenecks.\n\nMulti-streaming the GKR fractional sumcheck tree-building looks promising since bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel and precompute_m_build_partial_kernel together account for 95ms. The challenge is that GKR's sequential nature means multi-streaming only helps for independent operations within a single round, and if the kernel already saturates the SMs, there's limited gain. Alternatively, I could pre-compute and cache the fractional tree building results to avoid redundant computation across rounds.\n\nLooking at the Trace Commit bottleneck more carefully, the 253ms breaks down into stack_traces_into_expanded (16ms), the NTT/MLE interpolation kernels (_CT_NTT at 82ms, batch_ntt_kernel at 32ms, bit_rev_permutation_z at 33ms), and the Merkle tree row hashing plus compression. The NTT operations dominate this phase, so optimizing those kernels or finding ways to reduce the number of NTT calls could yield significant speedup. I'm trying a different approach by looking at the per-phase wall-clock timings from the spec.py output at APC 300, which breaks down the STARK execution time into Constraints, Openings, and Trace Commit phases with their respective sub-components.\n\nNow examining the same breakdown at APC 0, I notice the Trace Commit time nearly doubles from 253ms to 528ms, but that's actually a reasonable 2.09x scaling factor. The real bottlenecks appear elsewhere — Round 0 barely changes between the two settings, suggesting it's not the scaling problem I'm looking for.\n\nThe three components that aren't scaling properly (Round 0, MLE Rounds, and Stacked Reduction) total 365ms at APC 0 and 414ms at APC 300. If they scaled as well as other parts of the system, I could save around 232ms, which would push the total STARK time below 1.1 seconds and hit the 2x improvement target. However, this is essentially impossible to achieve because the AIR count jumps from 99 to 623 — a 6.3x increase — which directly drives the work in those components.\n\nSince I can't make per-AIR components scale with cell count, I need to either drastically reduce the per-AIR overhead through batching or fusion, focus on the largest absolute bottleneck like LogUp GKR at 529ms, or find other components where scaling could be improved. LogUp GKR is already close to 2x scaling, so pushing it to 2.5x would save another 113ms. Trace Commit is another candidate worth examining.\n\nThe most realistic path forward is targeting LogUp GKR first—saving 100ms there, then 50ms from Trace Commit, then 70ms from the Round 0 and MLE rounds combined. Now I'm digging into the GKR input evaluation to understand where the bottleneck actually is, since the prealloc-gkr-input-buffers optimization already showed a 1.67x improvement at APC 300.\n\nThe segments responded very differently to preallocation—seg0 barely improved at 1.07x while seg1 jumped 4.15x, which makes sense given that seg1 has many small allocations where mutex contention was the real problem. After that optimization, the input eval sits at around 318ms, leaving about 211ms for the GKR protocol itself.\n\nThe GPU kernels compress from 509ms down to 318ms across 8 streams, giving us a 1.6x ratio, but theoretically we could get much closer to 8x if everything parallelized perfectly. The issue is that a few large AIRs are creating a bottleneck that prevents better stream utilization.\n\nLooking at the kernel metrics, there are 486 instances of the main evaluation kernel with huge variance—most are under a millisecond but a handful exceed 10ms, and the largest hits 16.2ms. Those outliers are the \"tall and wide\" AIRs that saturate the GPU on their own, so when they land on a single stream, the other streams sit idle waiting for that one to finish.\n\nThe work distribution uses round-robin assignment across 8 threads, with each thread getting roughly 30 AIRs to process sequentially. The problem is that even though large AIRs are spread across different threads, if one thread draws a 16ms kernel while others finish in 2ms, that thread becomes the bottleneck for the entire batch.\n\nLooking at the numbers, the total GPU time is around 254ms per segment, which would ideally split to 32ms per thread with perfect parallelization, but we're seeing 160ms actual—about 5x overhead. The bottleneck comes down to a few large 13ms kernels that can't be subdivided further, combined with memory bandwidth constraints that prevent true concurrent execution on the GPU.\n\nFor the GKR input eval kernel specifically, I'm calculating that reading the full 3.2GB trace would only take about 3.2ms at the 4090's peak bandwidth of 1008 GB/s, yet the kernel runs for 509ms total. That means it's compute-bound, not memory-bound—the work involves reading trace columns, evaluating interaction expressions with field multiplications and additions, and accumulating numerator/denominator values. Since it's compute-bound rather than memory-bound, multi-streaming across the 4090's 128 SMs should theoretically help more, allowing separate kernels to occupy different SMs and improve overall utilization.\n\nThe real constraint emerges with larger AIRs: a single kernel launch can consume so many thread blocks that it occupies all available SMs, blocking other streams from executing concurrently. For instance, a TASK_SIZE=65536 with 256 threads per block creates 256 blocks that need 2 waves across the 128 SMs, and even larger AIRs with permutation heights around 2^17 generate 512 blocks requiring 4 full waves—completely saturating the GPU and preventing any concurrent execution from other streams. This SM saturation is the fundamental bottleneck preventing the multi-streaming strategy from delivering the expected speedup.\n\nThe `rightsize-gkr-input-kernel-grid` task attempted to address this by reducing grid sizes for smaller AIRs, but that approach failed because the large AIRs are the dominant consumers of resources. A better strategy would be to split large GKR input evaluation kernels into multiple smaller chunks distributed across streams, allowing smaller AIR kernels from other streams to interleave execution. However, even this faces a hard limit: a large AIR with height 2^17 already requires TASK_SIZE=65536, which splits into chunks of 256 blocks each—still saturating all 128 SMs per chunk, so further subdivision doesn't actually improve concurrency.\n\nReducing TASK_SIZE further would free up SM capacity and allow multiple chunks to run in parallel, but that change cascades into the intermediates buffer sizing and reduction logic, making it a more invasive refactor. Rather than continue down this path, I should examine the actual bottleneck more carefully by looking at the Trace Commit breakdown and understanding what's driving the cost in the rs_code_matrix function—specifically the scatter/expand operations, inverse NTT stages, and coefficient interpolation steps.\n\nThe RS encoding itself, which combines bit-reversal permutation and forward NTT over the expanded codeword dimensions, is showing up as a major contributor in the profiling data. The _CT_NTT kernel is running 50 times with significant variance in execution time, and the batch_ntt and bit_rev kernels together account for substantial overhead.\n\nLooking at the breakdown across all phases, the NTT-related operations total around 187ms, though I need to isolate which calls are specifically from the RS encoding matrix versus other components like stacked reduction or WHIR. The 25 instances of bit_rev_permutation_z seem to correlate with the 25 poseidon2 hash compressions, suggesting one permutation per stacked commitment layer.\n\nI'm realizing this level of granularity is difficult without proper profiling correlation. Let me step back and focus on concrete optimizations instead. My top candidate is caching the zero-row Merkle digests per tree height across stacked groups—since many groups share the same height, computing those empty node hashes once and reusing them could save 20-40ms with moderate complexity. Though actually, reviewing the Merkle tree construction more carefully, it builds from a single RS code matrix, so the zero-row caching approach might not apply as cleanly as I initially thought.\n\nThe second idea—overlapping GKR protocol computation with input evaluation—also runs into a constraint: the tree building kernel needs the entire input array before it can start, so there's no real opportunity to parallelize those phases.\n\nFor the third approach, I could split the NTT columns across multiple CUDA streams, but that would require restructuring the batch NTT kernel to handle subsets independently, which is complex and probably unnecessary since the NTT likely already maxes out memory bandwidth on the 4090.\n\nSo I'm shifting to a different angle entirely. Looking at how the CPU prover scales better with rayon—it distributes AIRs across cores with minimal per-AIR overhead since there are no kernel launches or GPU syncs. The GPU prover's bottleneck is that each AIR incurs launch overhead and synchronization costs that add up quickly.\n\nWhat if I batch multiple AIRs into a single kernel launch using a descriptor array? This worked for the MLE rounds but failed for Round 0 due to compiler issues. For GKR input eval, I could structure it so one kernel processes all AIRs at once, with each thread block handling one AIR and a descriptor telling it which trace and expression to use. The challenge is that each AIR has different trace pointers and heights, so the descriptor needs to encode all that variation efficiently. , width\n- Interaction expressions (different DAGs)\n- Number of interactions\n\nThe varying DAG structure makes batching harder. For the MLE rounds, all AIRs use the same kernel logic (fold, interpolate) — only the data pointers and dimensions differ. For GKR input eval, the expressions vary per AIR.\n\nWait, but looking at the GKR input eval kernel more carefully... There are only two variants: GLOBAL and SCATTER mode. Within GLOBAL mode, all AIRs use the same kernel but with different descriptor data (trace pointers, interaction rules, etc.). The rules are already encoded as device-side data (interaction rules uploaded to GPU).\n\nSo a batched GKR input eval kernel could use a descriptor array with per-AIR metadata and launch one kernel per batch, letting each thread block process one AIR based on its descriptor. This would eliminate per-AIR kernel launch overhead and let the GPU scheduler manage block-level concurrency automatically.\n\nBut the batch SCATTER mode approach already failed because GLOBAL AIRs dominate the workload. If I batched the GLOBAL-mode AIRs instead, I could combine ~486 kernel launches into a single launch. The challenge is that different AIRs have different heights affecting block count, each AIR has its own interaction rule DAG, and their traces live in different memory locations. I need to figure out how to handle variable grid sizes across descriptors in a single batch launch. could use a 1D grid with BlockCtx mapping (like the batched MLE round kernels). Each descriptor specifies how many blocks it needs, and blocks binary-search the descriptor array to find their work assignment.\n\nFor point 2: The interaction rules are variable-length and complex. We'd need to encode them in a flat GPU-accessible format, which the `precompute-logup-round0-interaction-rules` task already attempted for Round 0. That task found the overhead was only ~0.05-0.1ms per AIR, so the batching benefit might be small.\n\nFor point 3: Already handled via descriptors.\n\nThe main benefit of batching would be better GPU scheduling — with a single kernel launch of many blocks, the GPU scheduler can distribute them across SMs more efficiently than 8 sequential streams. The current multi-stream approach already achieves 1.6x compression (509ms → 318ms), but a batched approach might reach 2-3x compression (509ms → 170-250ms) by improving SM utilization when small AIR blocks run on otherwise idle SMs.\n\nThe challenge is that interaction rules form complex DAGs, making them non-trivial to encode for a batched kernel. But looking at the existing GKR input eval kernel, it already handles variable interaction rules via device pointers — a batched version would just need to index into the right rule set for each block.\n\nActually, let me reconsider a simpler alternative: reducing TASK_SIZE from 65536 to 16384 would decrease blocks per launch from 256 to 64, allowing 2 concurrent launches from different streams while also shrinking the intermediates buffer 4x per launch.\n\nBut this changes how the intermediates buffer is organized—with smaller TASK_SIZE, the reduction pattern shifts and more launches are needed overall. The kernel uses TASK_SIZE as the row count, with intermediates stored per thread with stride TASK_SIZE, so reducing it means fewer blocks per launch but more total launches required.\n\nThe tradeoff is clear: smaller TASK_SIZE increases CPU overhead from more launches but improves SM utilization per launch, enabling better inter-stream concurrency. The current 8-stream setup gets 1.6x compression, and optimizing per-launch efficiency could push that to 2.0-2.5x, potentially saving significant memory bandwidth.\n\nThe main uncertainty is whether the intermediates buffer and reduction logic can handle the smaller task granularity without major refactoring. I should check if TASK_SIZE is already configurable or hardcoded, then examine the actual GKR input eval implementation to assess feasibility.\n\nBefore diving deeper into that, though, I'm wondering if there's a better angle entirely—like using a dedicated background stream to overlap the Merkle tree's final D2H transfer with other work.\n\nActually, looking at how `to_host()` works, it uses the default per-thread stream, so the sync still blocks the entire pipeline. And from the earlier drain-gpu-pipeline investigation, by the time `prove()` runs, all GPU work is already done anyway, so this wouldn't help.\n\nI'm going to stop second-guessing myself and commit to the TASK_SIZE reduction approach—it's the most concrete optimization I've identified.\n\nWait, but there's another angle I should consider: what if I overlap the Constraints Phase Round 0 with the tail end of Trace Commit? The Merkle tree build needs the full digest layers for openings later, but the transcript observation only needs the root hash, and Round 0 constraint evaluation doesn't actually require the complete tree—just the root for the transcript. The constraint is that the transcript observation must happen before challenges are sampled for Round 0, but maybe there's still room to pipeline these operations.\n\nActually, that doesn't work either—the Merkle root is computed last, so there's nothing to overlap with since the commitment is the first operation in the prove flow.\n\nLet me shift focus: what if the bottleneck is the GKR input evaluation buffer allocation? The prealloc report shows these buffers consume `TASK_SIZE * num_intermediates * sizeof(FracExt)` per thread, which gets large with TASK_SIZE = 65536. The VPMM handles these allocations, but maybe the intermediates buffer itself is the constraint I should be targeting.\n\nThe memory access pattern is brutal—each thread writes to its intermediates slot with a stride of 2MB between consecutive writes within a block, which is terrible for GPU memory. I tried the warp-tiled-gkr-intermediates optimization to change from thread-interleaved to warp-tiled access, but it didn't help because trace reads dominate the bandwidth, not intermediates writes.\n\nI need to stop cycling through dead ends and commit to something concrete. Looking back at what actually worked across the 30 previous tasks, multi-streaming for per-AIR loops showed real gains.\n\nNow I'm reviewing what's left to try: batching kernel launches with descriptor arrays, pre-allocating buffers to avoid synchronization overhead, overlapping independent work, and GPU-side computation to cut down on host-device roundtrips. The untried approaches include multi-streaming for MLE rounds and Trace Commit, fusing multiple GKR protocol rounds, reducing TASK_SIZE for better concurrency, and batching GLOBAL-mode kernels.\n\nMulti-streaming the MLE rounds looks promising—with 623 AIRs at APC 300 being processed sequentially, there's real opportunity to parallelize across them.\n\nBut looking closer at the actual implementation, the MLE rounds already batch all traces into single kernel launches per operation, so multi-streaming wouldn't help since there's no per-trace kernel overhead to hide—the bottleneck is the kernel execution itself, not launch overhead. The number of MLE rounds is determined by the matrix height—APC 300 has fewer rounds (~15-17) than APC 0 (~17-19), yet MLE Rounds still runs slower at APC 300 (159ms vs 113ms). This is because APC 300 has significantly more AIRs (623 vs 99), which means more thread blocks per kernel launch despite smaller individual traces, driving up the computational work per round.\n\nSo the MLE kernel is already well-optimized—that 159ms is genuine computation. I'm reconsidering my approach and leaning toward optimizing the GKR input evaluation by reducing the per-AIR kernel overhead instead.\n\nLooking at the APC 300 breakdown more carefully, LogUp GKR dominates at 529ms, with the input evaluation taking about 310ms and the fractional sumcheck protocol another 210ms. Within the protocol itself, the compute_round_and_revert_kernel, bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel, and multifold_kernel are the main bottlenecks.\n\nThe 237ms of total GPU time compressing down to 210ms wall-clock suggests there's already some parallelization happening. I'm noticing that bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel appears 6 times across 50ms total—it's building the initial k=2 layers of the fractional binary tree from the input evaluations, where each element is represented as a numerator/denominator pair and the tree is constructed bottom-up.\n\nThe bit_rev_ prefix indicates bit-reversal permutation is happening during this process. With 6 instances running at APC 300, each takes roughly 8ms on average with some variance up to 20ms. The precompute_m_build_partial_kernel similarly runs 14 times at 45ms total, averaging 3.2ms per instance to build partial products for multilinear folding.\n\nThese are one-time setup costs per GKR invocation that don't scale with the number of AIRs—they process the entire tree once. Breaking down the 210ms wall-clock: roughly 62ms goes to setup (tree building and layer construction), leaving about 148ms for the per-round folding work across all rounds.\n\nLooking at the per-round breakdown, compute_round_and_revert dominates at 70ms, with multifold and precompute_m accounting for most of that. Across 14-15 outer rounds, that's roughly 12ms per round. Now I'm wondering whether any of this work could overlap with subsequent phases—the GKR protocol wraps up the Constraints phase, and then the MLE Rounds begin, so there might be opportunities to pipeline these stages.\n\nThe GKR input evaluation has to complete before the protocol can process the tree since it depends on all AIR inputs being ready, so no overlap there. Similarly, the MLE Rounds need the challenges output from GKR, so they can't start early either. The phases are fundamentally sequential.\n\nAt this point, the most promising avenue seems to be either optimizing the Trace Commit phase or rethinking the LogUp GKR approach entirely. Let me trace through the full STARK prover to see if there are any structural inefficiencies I'm missing.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3007,"cache_read_input_tokens":105682,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3007},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c4196e5-21f2-4e88-ab6f-fb2d2870d4db","uuid":"5fa095c1-a3d8-4403-85d7-af5cb7743e9b"}
