{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PywGerVZWAGJG5gqRSnfrG","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\n2\t  \"counter\": [\n3\t    {\n4\t      \"labels\": [\n5\t        [\n6\t          \"air_name\",\n7\t          \"ProgramAir\"\n8\t        ],\n9\t        [\n10\t          \"air_id\",\n11\t          \"0\"\n12\t        ]\n13\t      ],\n14\t      \"metric\": \"constraint_deg\",\n15\t      \"value\": \"1\"\n16\t    },\n17\t    {\n18\t      \"labels\": [\n19\t        [\n20\t          \"air_name\",\n21\t          \"ProgramAir\"\n22\t        ],\n23\t        [\n24\t          \"air_id\",\n25\t          \"0\"\n26\t        ]\n27\t      ],\n28\t      \"metric\": \"constraints\",\n29\t      \"value\": \"0\"\n30\t    },"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01NCF6c8mAruzi4vGafUmVoS","session_id":"5c4196e5-21f2-4e88-ab6f-fb2d2870d4db","uuid":"3686565c-7847-4b30-920e-69032c56e1fd","timestamp":"2026-04-14T23:04:47.841Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"abf32fa1da3c960c1","tool_use_id":"toolu_01NCF6c8mAruzi4vGafUmVoS","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"Review optimization plan","usage":{"total_tokens":79682,"tool_uses":54,"duration_ms":247684},"uuid":"73bc7654-7a95-4a76-a51f-b828f5c9b746","session_id":"5c4196e5-21f2-4e88-ab6f-fb2d2870d4db"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NCF6c8mAruzi4vGafUmVoS","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have everything needed to write the review. Let me check one final thing: the plan claims 529ms for LogUp GKR and 310ms for GKR input eval. But from the current metrics, seg0 shows input_evals = 248ms and seg1 = 62ms, totaling 310ms. LogUp GKR total is 361 + 168 = 529ms. This matches the plan's numbers.\n\nHere is the complete review.\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### 1. Descriptor struct is missing `buffer_size` and `intermediates_offset` (design blocker)\n\nThe `BatchGkrInputDesc` struct on line 52-64 of the plan omits two fields that are referenced in the plan's own narrative:\n\n- `buffer_size` -- needed by the kernel to compute each thread's intermediates scratch region size and to set `intermediate_stride = 1`. The current kernel (`gkr_input.cu:45`) reads `intermediates_ptr[z_index * intermediate_stride]`. With stride=1, the kernel still needs to know `buffer_size` to compute `intermediates_ptr = intermediates_base + air_offset + thread_local_id * buffer_size`.\n- `intermediates_offset` -- referenced at plan line 76 (\"desc.intermediates_offset\") and at plan line 108 (\"Compute intermediates_offset by prefix sum\") but never added to the struct definition.\n\nWithout these two fields, the kernel cannot compute per-thread intermediates pointers. The batched kernel signature at plan line 69 passes `intermediates_base` as a flat pointer, but there is no way for the kernel to find each AIR's intermediates region without `intermediates_offset` in the descriptor, and no way to stride within it without `buffer_size`.\n\n### 2. Different `buffer_size` values across AIRs are not addressed (design blocker)\n\nThe plan treats `buffer_size` as if it is uniform across all batch-eligible AIRs. However, `buffer_size` is per-AIR (`rules.inner.buffer_size`, line 158 of `gkr_input.rs`). Different AIRs may have different `buffer_size` values. The current single-AIR kernel does not need to handle this because each launch uses its own intermediates allocation sized to `TASK_SIZE * buffer_size`. In the batched kernel, the intermediates layout formula at plan line 79 (\"min(height, blocks_per_air * 256) * buffer_size\") is correct per-AIR, but the kernel needs to know each AIR's `buffer_size` at runtime to:\n- compute the per-thread intermediates pointer offset\n- pass `intermediate_stride = 1` to `evaluate_dag_entry_gkr` (which uses `z_index * intermediate_stride` to index into the scratch)\n\nIf `buffer_size` varies across AIRs, the contiguous intermediates region per AIR must account for each AIR's own value. This is handled correctly in the Rust-side prefix sum (plan line 108), but the CUDA kernel has no access to this information without `buffer_size` in the descriptor.\n\n### 3. Plan mischaracterizes the batch_mle.cu pattern (inaccuracy, not a blocker)\n\nPlan line 67 says the plan follows \"the same pattern as `batch_mle.cu`\" using binary search on `block_start`. This is incorrect. The existing `batch_mle.cu` uses a pre-built `BlockCtx` array (`d_block_ctxs[blockIdx.x]`, line 119 of `batch_mle.cu`) where each block index maps directly to a `{local_block_idx_x, air_idx}` pair -- there is no binary search. Binary search on `block_start` is used in `utils.cu` (lines 124-131) and `stacked_reduction.cu` (lines 475-486). Either approach works, but the plan should reference the correct pattern source.\n\n### 4. The plan's expected 40-80ms saving is not supported by historical measurements (performance realism concern)\n\nThree prior attempts targeted the same GKR input eval phase with similar mechanisms:\n\n- **Right-sizing kernel grid** (`2026-04-14-0030`): Used `min(TASK_SIZE, height)` to reduce idle threads. Result: 0ms improvement (noise). The plan's batched kernel also right-sizes block counts per AIR, which is the same fundamental mechanism.\n- **Warp-tiled intermediates** (`2026-04-14-1430`): Changed intermediate stride from 65536 to 32 for better cache locality. Result: 0ms improvement. The plan proposes stride=1, which is an even more aggressive locality change that the same kernel showed no benefit from.\n- **Batch SCATTER GKR input** (`2026-04-14-0830`): Batched ~305 SCATTER-mode AIRs into one launch, eliminating per-AIR launch overhead. Result: -8ms (below threshold, reverted).\n\nThe plan combines right-sizing (proven to give 0ms) with intermediates locality improvement (proven to give 0ms) with kernel launch batching (proven to give -8ms for SCATTER). The novelty is that GLOBAL AIRs are more numerous and each launches a larger kernel (256 blocks vs 4-16 for SCATTER), so batching may have more impact by eliminating 256-block launches that briefly occupy all 128 SMs. However, the plan estimates 40-80ms from \"improved compression to 2.0-2.5x\" without evidence that the current 1.64x compression ratio is caused by per-launch SM monopolization rather than, say, kernel execution time itself.\n\nThe current input_evals timing at APC 300 is 248ms (seg0) + 62ms (seg1) = 310ms. A 40-80ms improvement would be a 13-26% reduction. Given that the plan targets the same phase where three prior optimizations yielded 0ms, the estimate appears optimistic.\n\n### 5. The tile-loop exclusion criterion is more subtle than stated (potential correctness issue)\n\nPlan line 103 says batch-eligible AIRs have `height <= TASK_SIZE` and `num_rows_per_tile == 1`. This is correct for the current code: `num_rows_per_tile = height.div_ceil(TASK_SIZE as usize).max(1)` (line 162 of `gkr_input.rs`), so `height <= 65536` implies `num_rows_per_tile == 1`.\n\nThe plan states (invariant 4, line 134) that large AIRs (`height > TASK_SIZE`) \"require intermediates at task_stride width, which doesn't fit the batched layout.\" This reasoning is correct: the tile loop (j=0,1,...) reuses the same intermediates offset for different rows, so intermediates must be at `task_stride` width to avoid aliasing. With stride=1, the intermediates would alias across tile iterations. The exclusion is correct.\n\nHowever, the plan does not state what fraction of the 300 GLOBAL AIRs have `height <= TASK_SIZE` at APC 300. If most GLOBAL AIRs have heights in the 256-65536 range (as claimed at plan line 36), the coverage is likely good. But if a significant fraction has `height > 65536`, the batched path may handle too few AIRs to matter.\n\n### 6. Post-processing synchronization is correctly identified but adds overhead (non-blocker)\n\nPlan line 136 correctly identifies that AIRs with `height != lifted_height` need post-processing after the batched kernel completes. The plan proposes `current_stream_sync()` after the batched launch, then sequential normalization per eligible AIR. This is correct but introduces a synchronization point that was not present in the multi-stream path (where each thread's normalization ran asynchronously on its own stream). For the batched path this sync is necessary, but it means the normalization kernels run sequentially on the main stream rather than concurrently across 8 streams.\n\n### 7. The `d_fracs` output pointer in the descriptor is semantically different from the kernel parameter (non-blocker)\n\nThe current kernel takes `d_fracs` as a flat pointer and computes output offset as `interaction_idx * permutation_height + row` (line 203 of `gkr_input.cu`). The plan's descriptor has `d_fracs` as \"output base for this AIR.\" The Rust code at `gkr_input.rs:241` computes `leaves_ptr = leaves.as_mut_ptr().add(dst_offset)` based on the stacked layout slice. This means each AIR's `d_fracs` pointer is already pre-offset. The kernel indexing `interaction_idx * permutation_height + row` relative to this pre-offset pointer is correct and matches current behavior. This is fine.\n\n## Improvements I would consider\n\n### 1. Use the BlockCtx pattern instead of binary search\n\nThe existing `batch_mle.cu` uses a pre-built `BlockCtx` array (`d_block_ctxs[blockIdx.x]`) where the Rust side pre-computes the mapping of block index to `{local_block_idx_x, air_idx}`. This avoids the O(log N) binary search per block and is trivial to implement since the Rust side already iterates over AIRs to build descriptors. Given that N can be ~300, the binary search adds ~9 comparisons per block, which is small but the direct-index approach is simpler and already proven in this codebase.\n\n### 2. Profile the actual inter-kernel gap before committing to this plan\n\nThe plan claims 1.64x compression (509ms GPU time -> 310ms wall) and assumes the gap is caused by poor stream-level interleaving. An `nsys` trace showing the actual inter-kernel idle gaps on the SMs would confirm whether the ~200ms of unexplained wall time is from SM idle periods (fixable by batching) or from CPU-side work like H2D uploads, public_values copies, and partition_ptrs uploads (not fixable by batching). The three prior failed attempts suggest the latter.\n\n### 3. Consider overlapping the batched kernel with multi-stream fallback\n\nInstead of running the batched kernel first on the main thread and then spawning workers for large AIRs (plan's Phase 3), the batched kernel could run on a dedicated stream concurrent with the multi-stream workers for large AIRs. This would avoid the synchronization point that blocks the workers while the batched kernel runs.\n\n## Assessment\n\nThere are two remaining design-level blockers:\n\n1. The `BatchGkrInputDesc` struct is missing `buffer_size` and `intermediates_offset` fields. Without these, the CUDA kernel cannot compute per-thread intermediates pointers. This is a straightforward fix (add both fields to the struct and the Rust repr(C) mirror).\n\n2. The plan does not specify how varying `buffer_size` across AIRs affects the intermediates layout. The kernel needs `buffer_size` per descriptor to index intermediates correctly. This is also fixable by adding `buffer_size` to the descriptor.\n\nAfter fixing these, the plan is prototype-ready. The checklist is sufficient for implementation -- the call chain is correctly traced, the output independence property is correctly identified, the tile-loop exclusion is sound, and the post-processing handling is correct.\n\nThe weakest part of the plan is the expected savings estimate (40-80ms). Three prior optimizations targeting the same kernel phase -- right-sizing (0ms), intermediates locality (0ms), and SCATTER batching (-8ms) -- all failed to produce measurable improvement. The plan's batching of GLOBAL AIRs is a different mechanism (eliminating ~300 full-grid launches vs. ~305 tiny launches), but the historical evidence suggests that kernel launch overhead and SM scheduling inefficiency are not the dominant cost in this phase at APC 300. A 20ms improvement is more plausible than 40-80ms.\n\nThe strongest part of the plan is the output independence analysis (plan lines 42-43 and invariants 1-3). The observation that each row writes to a unique position in `d_fracs` with no cross-thread reduction is correct and necessary for batching correctness. The intermediates isolation analysis (per-thread scratch rewritten every tile iteration) is also sound."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: abf32fa1da3c960c1 (use SendMessage with to: 'abf32fa1da3c960c1' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 81588\ntool_uses: 54\nduration_ms: 247684</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c4196e5-21f2-4e88-ab6f-fb2d2870d4db","uuid":"229aa5a3-5858-49a7-8263-9fcb83525d79","timestamp":"2026-04-14T23:06:01.542Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Review this optimization plan following the review guideline. Return your response in the format: Findings, Improvements, Assessment.\n\nYou need to read the following files and review the plan against the current codebase:\n\n1. `autoopt/review_guideline.md` — the review standard to follow\n2. `autoopt/context.md` — project context  \n3. `autoopt-results/task.md` — the task being planned\n4. `autoopt-results/2026-04-15-0100-batch-global-gkr-input-eval/plan.md` — the plan to review\n\nThen check the actual code at:\n- `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.cu` — the CUDA kernel being modified\n- `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs` — the Rust orchestration code\n- `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs` — the FFI bindings\n- `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.cu` — existing batched kernel pattern to compare against\n\nVerify that:\n1. The plan matches the actual code architecture (function signatures, data structures, kernel parameters)\n2. The claimed bottleneck is real and the proposed mechanism addresses it\n3. The intermediates layout change is correct and doesn't break DAG evaluation\n4. The descriptor struct covers all data the kernel needs\n5. The threshold (height ≤ TASK_SIZE) correctly partitions eligible vs non-eligible AIRs\n6. The post-processing (height != lifted_height normalization) is correctly handled\n7. The expected savings are realistic given the nsight profiling data\n\nBe strict about design-level correctness issues. Reference specific file paths and line numbers.","agentId":"abf32fa1da3c960c1","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have everything needed to write the review. Let me check one final thing: the plan claims 529ms for LogUp GKR and 310ms for GKR input eval. But from the current metrics, seg0 shows input_evals = 248ms and seg1 = 62ms, totaling 310ms. LogUp GKR total is 361 + 168 = 529ms. This matches the plan's numbers.\n\nHere is the complete review.\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### 1. Descriptor struct is missing `buffer_size` and `intermediates_offset` (design blocker)\n\nThe `BatchGkrInputDesc` struct on line 52-64 of the plan omits two fields that are referenced in the plan's own narrative:\n\n- `buffer_size` -- needed by the kernel to compute each thread's intermediates scratch region size and to set `intermediate_stride = 1`. The current kernel (`gkr_input.cu:45`) reads `intermediates_ptr[z_index * intermediate_stride]`. With stride=1, the kernel still needs to know `buffer_size` to compute `intermediates_ptr = intermediates_base + air_offset + thread_local_id * buffer_size`.\n- `intermediates_offset` -- referenced at plan line 76 (\"desc.intermediates_offset\") and at plan line 108 (\"Compute intermediates_offset by prefix sum\") but never added to the struct definition.\n\nWithout these two fields, the kernel cannot compute per-thread intermediates pointers. The batched kernel signature at plan line 69 passes `intermediates_base` as a flat pointer, but there is no way for the kernel to find each AIR's intermediates region without `intermediates_offset` in the descriptor, and no way to stride within it without `buffer_size`.\n\n### 2. Different `buffer_size` values across AIRs are not addressed (design blocker)\n\nThe plan treats `buffer_size` as if it is uniform across all batch-eligible AIRs. However, `buffer_size` is per-AIR (`rules.inner.buffer_size`, line 158 of `gkr_input.rs`). Different AIRs may have different `buffer_size` values. The current single-AIR kernel does not need to handle this because each launch uses its own intermediates allocation sized to `TASK_SIZE * buffer_size`. In the batched kernel, the intermediates layout formula at plan line 79 (\"min(height, blocks_per_air * 256) * buffer_size\") is correct per-AIR, but the kernel needs to know each AIR's `buffer_size` at runtime to:\n- compute the per-thread intermediates pointer offset\n- pass `intermediate_stride = 1` to `evaluate_dag_entry_gkr` (which uses `z_index * intermediate_stride` to index into the scratch)\n\nIf `buffer_size` varies across AIRs, the contiguous intermediates region per AIR must account for each AIR's own value. This is handled correctly in the Rust-side prefix sum (plan line 108), but the CUDA kernel has no access to this information without `buffer_size` in the descriptor.\n\n### 3. Plan mischaracterizes the batch_mle.cu pattern (inaccuracy, not a blocker)\n\nPlan line 67 says the plan follows \"the same pattern as `batch_mle.cu`\" using binary search on `block_start`. This is incorrect. The existing `batch_mle.cu` uses a pre-built `BlockCtx` array (`d_block_ctxs[blockIdx.x]`, line 119 of `batch_mle.cu`) where each block index maps directly to a `{local_block_idx_x, air_idx}` pair -- there is no binary search. Binary search on `block_start` is used in `utils.cu` (lines 124-131) and `stacked_reduction.cu` (lines 475-486). Either approach works, but the plan should reference the correct pattern source.\n\n### 4. The plan's expected 40-80ms saving is not supported by historical measurements (performance realism concern)\n\nThree prior attempts targeted the same GKR input eval phase with similar mechanisms:\n\n- **Right-sizing kernel grid** (`2026-04-14-0030`): Used `min(TASK_SIZE, height)` to reduce idle threads. Result: 0ms improvement (noise). The plan's batched kernel also right-sizes block counts per AIR, which is the same fundamental mechanism.\n- **Warp-tiled intermediates** (`2026-04-14-1430`): Changed intermediate stride from 65536 to 32 for better cache locality. Result: 0ms improvement. The plan proposes stride=1, which is an even more aggressive locality change that the same kernel showed no benefit from.\n- **Batch SCATTER GKR input** (`2026-04-14-0830`): Batched ~305 SCATTER-mode AIRs into one launch, eliminating per-AIR launch overhead. Result: -8ms (below threshold, reverted).\n\nThe plan combines right-sizing (proven to give 0ms) with intermediates locality improvement (proven to give 0ms) with kernel launch batching (proven to give -8ms for SCATTER). The novelty is that GLOBAL AIRs are more numerous and each launches a larger kernel (256 blocks vs 4-16 for SCATTER), so batching may have more impact by eliminating 256-block launches that briefly occupy all 128 SMs. However, the plan estimates 40-80ms from \"improved compression to 2.0-2.5x\" without evidence that the current 1.64x compression ratio is caused by per-launch SM monopolization rather than, say, kernel execution time itself.\n\nThe current input_evals timing at APC 300 is 248ms (seg0) + 62ms (seg1) = 310ms. A 40-80ms improvement would be a 13-26% reduction. Given that the plan targets the same phase where three prior optimizations yielded 0ms, the estimate appears optimistic.\n\n### 5. The tile-loop exclusion criterion is more subtle than stated (potential correctness issue)\n\nPlan line 103 says batch-eligible AIRs have `height <= TASK_SIZE` and `num_rows_per_tile == 1`. This is correct for the current code: `num_rows_per_tile = height.div_ceil(TASK_SIZE as usize).max(1)` (line 162 of `gkr_input.rs`), so `height <= 65536` implies `num_rows_per_tile == 1`.\n\nThe plan states (invariant 4, line 134) that large AIRs (`height > TASK_SIZE`) \"require intermediates at task_stride width, which doesn't fit the batched layout.\" This reasoning is correct: the tile loop (j=0,1,...) reuses the same intermediates offset for different rows, so intermediates must be at `task_stride` width to avoid aliasing. With stride=1, the intermediates would alias across tile iterations. The exclusion is correct.\n\nHowever, the plan does not state what fraction of the 300 GLOBAL AIRs have `height <= TASK_SIZE` at APC 300. If most GLOBAL AIRs have heights in the 256-65536 range (as claimed at plan line 36), the coverage is likely good. But if a significant fraction has `height > 65536`, the batched path may handle too few AIRs to matter.\n\n### 6. Post-processing synchronization is correctly identified but adds overhead (non-blocker)\n\nPlan line 136 correctly identifies that AIRs with `height != lifted_height` need post-processing after the batched kernel completes. The plan proposes `current_stream_sync()` after the batched launch, then sequential normalization per eligible AIR. This is correct but introduces a synchronization point that was not present in the multi-stream path (where each thread's normalization ran asynchronously on its own stream). For the batched path this sync is necessary, but it means the normalization kernels run sequentially on the main stream rather than concurrently across 8 streams.\n\n### 7. The `d_fracs` output pointer in the descriptor is semantically different from the kernel parameter (non-blocker)\n\nThe current kernel takes `d_fracs` as a flat pointer and computes output offset as `interaction_idx * permutation_height + row` (line 203 of `gkr_input.cu`). The plan's descriptor has `d_fracs` as \"output base for this AIR.\" The Rust code at `gkr_input.rs:241` computes `leaves_ptr = leaves.as_mut_ptr().add(dst_offset)` based on the stacked layout slice. This means each AIR's `d_fracs` pointer is already pre-offset. The kernel indexing `interaction_idx * permutation_height + row` relative to this pre-offset pointer is correct and matches current behavior. This is fine.\n\n## Improvements I would consider\n\n### 1. Use the BlockCtx pattern instead of binary search\n\nThe existing `batch_mle.cu` uses a pre-built `BlockCtx` array (`d_block_ctxs[blockIdx.x]`) where the Rust side pre-computes the mapping of block index to `{local_block_idx_x, air_idx}`. This avoids the O(log N) binary search per block and is trivial to implement since the Rust side already iterates over AIRs to build descriptors. Given that N can be ~300, the binary search adds ~9 comparisons per block, which is small but the direct-index approach is simpler and already proven in this codebase.\n\n### 2. Profile the actual inter-kernel gap before committing to this plan\n\nThe plan claims 1.64x compression (509ms GPU time -> 310ms wall) and assumes the gap is caused by poor stream-level interleaving. An `nsys` trace showing the actual inter-kernel idle gaps on the SMs would confirm whether the ~200ms of unexplained wall time is from SM idle periods (fixable by batching) or from CPU-side work like H2D uploads, public_values copies, and partition_ptrs uploads (not fixable by batching). The three prior failed attempts suggest the latter.\n\n### 3. Consider overlapping the batched kernel with multi-stream fallback\n\nInstead of running the batched kernel first on the main thread and then spawning workers for large AIRs (plan's Phase 3), the batched kernel could run on a dedicated stream concurrent with the multi-stream workers for large AIRs. This would avoid the synchronization point that blocks the workers while the batched kernel runs.\n\n## Assessment\n\nThere are two remaining design-level blockers:\n\n1. The `BatchGkrInputDesc` struct is missing `buffer_size` and `intermediates_offset` fields. Without these, the CUDA kernel cannot compute per-thread intermediates pointers. This is a straightforward fix (add both fields to the struct and the Rust repr(C) mirror).\n\n2. The plan does not specify how varying `buffer_size` across AIRs affects the intermediates layout. The kernel needs `buffer_size` per descriptor to index intermediates correctly. This is also fixable by adding `buffer_size` to the descriptor.\n\nAfter fixing these, the plan is prototype-ready. The checklist is sufficient for implementation -- the call chain is correctly traced, the output independence property is correctly identified, the tile-loop exclusion is sound, and the post-processing handling is correct.\n\nThe weakest part of the plan is the expected savings estimate (40-80ms). Three prior optimizations targeting the same kernel phase -- right-sizing (0ms), intermediates locality (0ms), and SCATTER batching (-8ms) -- all failed to produce measurable improvement. The plan's batching of GLOBAL AIRs is a different mechanism (eliminating ~300 full-grid launches vs. ~305 tiny launches), but the historical evidence suggests that kernel launch overhead and SM scheduling inefficiency are not the dominant cost in this phase at APC 300. A 20ms improvement is more plausible than 40-80ms.\n\nThe strongest part of the plan is the output independence analysis (plan lines 42-43 and invariants 1-3). The observation that each row writes to a unique position in `d_fracs` with no cross-thread reduction is correct and necessary for batching correctness. The intermediates isolation analysis (per-thread scratch rewritten every tile iteration) is also sound."}],"totalDurationMs":247684,"totalTokens":81588,"totalToolUseCount":54,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":327,"cache_read_input_tokens":78355,"output_tokens":2905,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":327},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":2905,"cache_read_input_tokens":78355,"cache_creation_input_tokens":327,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":327,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":24,"searchCount":25,"bashCount":5,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
