.x`\n- Guard: `if (row >= desc.permutation_height) return`\n- Compute intermediates pointer: `FpExt *my_intermediates = intermediates_base + desc.intermediates_offset + row * desc.buffer_size`\n- Set `intermediate_stride = 1` (contiguous per-thread scratch)\n- Execute the same DAG evaluation loop as `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel` (lines 98-206 of current kernel)\n- Write output to `desc.d_fracs` using `desc.permutation_height` for indexing\n\n**Intermediates layout**: Each batch-eligible AIR allocates `height * buffer_size` entries in the shared intermediates buffer. Thread at row `r` uses entries `[intermediates_offset + r * buffer_size, intermediates_offset + (r+1) * buffer_size)`. Since different AIRs have different `buffer_size` values, the per-AIR allocation varies. The Rust side computes `intermediates_offset` via prefix sum over `height * buffer_size` for each eligible AIR.\n\n**Why stride=1 is correct for batch-eligible AIRs**: These AIRs have `num_rows_per_tile == 1`, meaning each thread processes exactly ONE row and exits. The intermediates are used only within that single row evaluation — no reuse across tile iterations. With stride=1, consecutive DAG node results for the same thread are contiguous, improving L1 cache locality vs the current stride=65536 layout.\n\nAdd a C launcher: `_batched_gkr_input_eval(const BatchGkrInputDesc *descs, const GkrInputBlockCtx *block_ctxs, uint32_t total_blocks, FpExt *intermediates, const FpExt *d_challenges)`.\n\n### 2. Rust FFI bindings (`crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`)\n\nAdd:\n- `#[repr(C)] struct BatchGkrInputDesc` matching the CUDA struct (with raw pointer fields)\n- `#[repr(C)] struct GkrInputBlockCtx { local_block_idx: u32, air_idx: u32 }`\n- `unsafe impl Send for BatchGkrInputDesc` + `Sync`\n- `unsafe impl Send for GkrInputBlockCtx` + `Sync`\n- `extern \"C\" fn _batched_gkr_input_eval(descs: *const BatchGkrInputDesc, block_ctxs: *const GkrInputBlockCtx, total_blocks: u32, intermediates: *mut EF, d_challenges: *const EF) -> i32`\n- Safe wrapper `batched_gkr_input_eval_gpu(d_descs: &DeviceBuffer<BatchGkrInputDesc>, d_block_ctxs: &DeviceBuffer<GkrInputBlockCtx>, intermediates: &mut DeviceBuffer<EF>, d_challenges: &DeviceBuffer<EF>)`\n\n### 3. Rust orchestration (`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`)\n\nModify `log_gkr_input_evals()`:\n\n**Phase 1 (extended)**: When building work items, also collect per-AIR metadata needed for the batched path:\n- `is_global` flag (from `buffer_size > 10`)\n- `buffer_size` value\n- `height`\n- For GLOBAL AIRs with `height ≤ TASK_SIZE` (i.e., `num_rows_per_tile == 1`): mark as `batch_eligible`\n\n**New Phase 1.5 — Build batched descriptors for eligible AIRs**:\n1. Partition work items into `batch_eligible` and `non_eligible` lists\n2. For each batch-eligible AIR:\n   - Compute `blocks_per_air = height.div_ceil(256)` (matches kernel block size of 256)\n   - Compute `intermediates_per_air = height * buffer_size` (in EF elements)\n3. Prefix-sum `block_start` values: `block_start[i] = sum(blocks_per_air[0..i])`\n4. Prefix-sum `intermediates_offset` values: `offset[i] = sum(intermediates_per_air[0..i])`\n5. Build `Vec<BatchGkrInputDesc>` with all per-AIR pointers and metadata\n6. Build `Vec<GkrInputBlockCtx>` by iterating: for each AIR `a`, for `b` in `0..blocks_per_air[a]`, push `GkrInputBlockCtx { local_block_idx: b, air_idx: a }`\n7. Collect per-AIR `partition_ptrs` into a flat `Vec<u64>`, upload once via `to_device()`. Each descriptor's `d_main` points into this buffer.\n8. Collect per-AIR `public_values` into a flat `Vec<F>`, upload once. Each descriptor's `d_public_values` points into this buffer (or null if empty).\n9. Upload `descs.to_device()`, `block_ctxs.to_device()`\n10. Allocate shared intermediates: `DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(total_intermediates)`\n\n**Phase 3 (modified)**: Run batched and multi-stream paths concurrently:\n- Launch the batched kernel on a **dedicated background stream** (not the main thread's stream) so it runs concurrently with the multi-stream workers\n- Spawn worker threads for non-eligible AIRs (large GLOBAL + all SCATTER) using existing `process_gkr_input_air()` logic\n- After all workers join, sync the background stream to ensure batched kernel completed\n- Then run height-normalization (`frac_vector_scalar_multiply_ext_fp` + `frac_matrix_vertically_repeat`) for batch-eligible AIRs that need it (where `height != lifted_height`)\n\n**APC 0 behavior**: At APC 0, `num_threads = 1` (< 100 AIRs). The batched kernel still runs for eligible GLOBAL AIRs, replacing sequential per-AIR launches. Non-eligible AIRs run on the main thread sequentially. This should be at least as fast.\n\n### 4. Handle per-AIR data on device\n\n- **Rules, used_nodes, pair_idxs**: Already on device as `pk_air.other_data.interaction_rules.inner.d_rules` etc. (uploaded at keygen time). Descriptors store raw pointers to these existing device buffers.\n- **partition_ptrs**: Currently uploaded per-AIR via `partition_ptrs.copy_to(&mut buffers.partition_ptrs)`. For the batched path, collect all partition pointer lists into one flat device buffer, with each descriptor pointing to its section.\n- **public_values**: Same pattern — flat buffer with per-AIR offsets.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Output correctness**: Each row in `d_fracs` must produce the same value as the per-AIR kernel. The DAG evaluation logic is identical; only the launch geometry and intermediates layout change.\n2. **No cross-AIR interference**: Each AIR's blocks write to disjoint regions of `d_fracs` (guaranteed by different `leaves_ptr` offsets from the stacked layout).\n3. **Intermediates isolation**: Each thread's scratch is at a unique offset: `intermediates_offset + row * buffer_size` for `row < permutation_height`. No two threads share scratch space. Different AIRs have different `intermediates_offset` values (prefix sum ensures non-overlap). Different rows within an AIR have different `row` values.\n4. **Varying buffer_size**: The `buffer_size` field in each descriptor tells the kernel how many EF elements to stride per row. The intermediates offset computation on the Rust side uses each AIR's own `buffer_size` to compute the prefix sum correctly.\n5. **Fallback for large AIRs**: AIRs with `height > TASK_SIZE` (`num_rows_per_tile > 1`) continue using the existing per-AIR kernel on worker threads. The tile loop in those kernels reuses intermediates across tiles (same thread processes multiple rows), requiring the current `task_stride` layout. The batched kernel's stride=1 layout only works when `num_rows_per_tile == 1`.\n6. **APC 0 safety**: At APC 0 (99 AIRs, below multi-stream threshold), the batched kernel replaces sequential per-AIR launches. Non-eligible AIRs run sequentially. No regression expected.\n7. **Height normalization**: Eligible AIRs where `height != lifted_height` need post-processing after the batched kernel. Sync the background stream first, then run normalization kernels on the main stream.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n1. Build: `cargo check -p openvm-cuda-backend`\n2. Test: `cargo nextest run -p openvm-cuda-backend --test-threads=4`\n3. Benchmark APC 0: `cd /home/georg/powdr && openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh` (APC 0 only)\n4. Benchmark APC 300: same script, APC 300\n5. Compare:\n   - LogUp GKR at APC 300: expect 529ms → ~490-510ms\n   - STARK excl trace at APC 300: expect 1301ms → ~1260-1280ms\n   - No regression at APC 0\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\n- Less than 20ms improvement on STARK excl trace at APC 300\n- Any regression at APC 0 (STARK excl trace increase > 15ms)\n- Test failures\n- cudaErrorIllegalAddress or other CUDA errors\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":2,"oldLines":8,"newStart":2,"newLines":10,"lines":[" "," ## Goal"," ","-Replace per-AIR GLOBAL-mode GKR input evaluation kernel launches with a single batched descriptor-array kernel for small AIRs (height ≤ TASK_SIZE). This targets the largest STARK excl trace component: LogUp GKR at 529ms (41% of 1301ms total). The GKR input eval accounts for ~310ms of wall-clock time with 509ms of GPU kernel time compressed to 310ms via 8-stream multi-threading (1.64x compression). By using GPU-native block scheduling instead of coarse-grained stream scheduling, we expect to improve compression to ~2.0-2.5x, saving 40-80ms.","+Replace per-AIR GLOBAL-mode GKR input evaluation kernel launches with a single batched descriptor-array kernel for small AIRs (height ≤ TASK_SIZE). This targets the largest STARK excl trace component: LogUp GKR at 529ms (41% of 1301ms total). The GKR input eval accounts for ~310ms of wall-clock time with 509ms of GPU kernel time compressed to 310ms via 8-stream multi-threading (1.64x compression). By using GPU-native block scheduling instead of coarse-grained stream scheduling and eliminating ~300 full-grid (256-block) kernel launches per segment, we expect to save 20-50ms from LogUp GKR."," ","+**Calibration against prior work**: Three prior optimizations targeted this same phase — right-sizing grid (0ms), intermediates locality change (0ms), and SCATTER batching (-8ms). The key difference here is that GLOBAL AIRs each launch 256 blocks (vs 4-16 for SCATTER), and there are ~300 such launches per segment, each briefly monopolizing all 128 SMs for 2 waves. This is a fundamentally different overhead profile than the SCATTER case.","+"," ## Current Code Path"," "," ### Entry point"]},{"oldStart":17,"oldLines":7,"newStart":19,"newLines":7,"lines":[" 5. **Worker loop**: Each thread processes its assigned work items sequentially via `process_gkr_input_air()` (line 112)"," 6. **Per-AIR processing** (line 112-209): For each AIR:","    - Collect trace pointers, public values, interaction rules","-   - Determine GLOBAL vs SCATTER mode (`buffer_size > 10`)","+   - Determine GLOBAL vs SCATTER mode (`buffer_size > 10`, line 160)","    - Launch `_logup_gkr_input_eval` CUDA kernel with `count = TASK_SIZE = 65536` for GLOBAL mode"," "," ### CUDA kernel"]},{"oldStart":25,"oldLines":115,"newStart":27,"newLines":130,"lines":[" "," For GLOBAL mode:"," - **Grid**: 256 blocks × 256 threads = 65536 threads (always, regardless of AIR height)","-- **Intermediates**: global memory at stride `task_stride = gridDim.x * blockDim.x`","+- **Intermediates**: global memory, `intermediates_ptr = d_intermediates + task_offset`, `intermediate_stride = task_stride = gridDim.x * blockDim.x`"," - **Tile loop**: each thread processes `num_rows_per_tile` rows: `row = task_offset + j * task_stride`","-- **Output**: writes to `d_fracs[interaction_idx * permutation_height + row]` — per-row, no cross-thread reduction","+- **Output**: writes to `d_fracs[(interaction_idx * permutation_height + row) * 2 + is_denom]` — per-row, no cross-thread reduction"," "," ### Why it's slow"," At APC 300 (2 segments, 623 AIR instances):","-- ~300 GLOBAL-mode AIRs per segment","+- ~300 GLOBAL-mode AIRs per segment (those with `buffer_size > 10`)"," - Each launches a 256-block kernel (65536 threads)"," - Small AIRs (height 256-4096) use <6% of launched threads; the rest check `row < permutation_height` and exit","-- 8-stream multi-threading achieves only 1.64x compression (509ms → 310ms) because:","-  - Each 256-block launch briefly occupies all 128 SMs (2 waves), blocking other streams","-  - The GPU scheduler can only interleave kernels between launches, not within a launch","-  - Per-launch overhead (kernel launch API ~5μs × 300 launches = ~1.5ms per segment) adds up","+- 8-stream multi-threading achieves only 1.64x compression (509ms GPU → 310ms wall) because:","+  - Each 256-block launch briefly occupies all 128 SMs (2 waves), blocking other streams' kernels","+  - Per-launch overhead (~5μs × 300 launches = ~1.5ms per segment) and CPU-side per-AIR setup (H2D for partition_ptrs, public_values) add up","+  - CPU iteration to collect per-AIR metadata before each launch"," "," ### Key property enabling batching","-**Output independence**: Each row's output goes to a unique position in `d_fracs`. There is NO cross-row or cross-thread reduction. Intermediates are per-thread scratch (rewritten for each row in the tile loop). This means different AIRs' computations are fully independent and can share a kernel launch.","+**Output independence**: Each row's output goes to a unique position in `d_fracs`. There is NO cross-row or cross-thread reduction. Intermediates are per-thread scratch (rewritten for each row in the tile loop, since `num_rows_per_tile == 1` for batch-eligible AIRs). This means different AIRs' computations are fully independent and can share a kernel launch."," "," ## Changes"," "," ### 1. CUDA batched kernel (`crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.cu`)"," ","-Add a descriptor struct and batched kernel:","+Add a descriptor struct:"," "," ```c"," struct BatchGkrInputDesc {","-    FracExt *d_fracs;          // output base for this AIR","-    const Fp *d_preprocessed;  // preprocessed trace","-    const uint64_t *d_main;    // partition pointers","-    const Fp *d_public_values; // public values","-    const Rule *d_rules;       // interaction rules","-    const size_t *d_used_nodes;","-    const uint32_t *d_pair_idxs;","-    size_t used_nodes_len;","-    uint32_t permutation_height;","-    uint32_t block_start;      // first block for this AIR","-    uint32_t block_end;        // exclusive end block for this AIR","+    FracExt *d_fracs;           // output base for this AIR (pre-offset by stacked layout)","+    const Fp *d_preprocessed;   // preprocessed trace buffer","+    const uint64_t *d_main;     // partition pointers (device)","+    const Fp *d_public_values;  // public values (device, may be null)","+    const Rule *d_rules;        // interaction rules","+    const size_t *d_used_nodes; // used nodes array","+    const uint32_t *d_pair_idxs;// pair index array","+    size_t used_nodes_len;      // length of used_nodes","+    uint32_t permutation_height;// this AIR's height","+    uint32_t buffer_size;       // intermediates scratch size per thread for this AIR","+    uint32_t intermediates_offset; // offset into shared intermediates buffer (in EF elements)","+    uint32_t block_start;       // first block index for this AIR (for BlockCtx lookup)"," };"," ```"," ","-Add a `BlockCtx` that maps `blockIdx.x → (air_desc_index, local_block_id)` via binary search on `block_start` (same pattern as `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.cu`).","+Add a `BlockCtx` array (one entry per block, pre-built on Rust side) following the pattern in `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.cu:119`:"," ","-New kernel: `batched_gkr_input_eval_kernel(const BatchGkrInputDesc *descs, uint32_t num_descs, FpExt *intermediates_base, const FpExt *d_challenges)`","+```c","+struct GkrInputBlockCtx {","+    uint32_t local_block_idx;  // block index within this AIR","+    uint32_t air_idx;          // index into BatchGkrInputDesc array","+};","+```"," ","+New kernel: `batched_gkr_input_eval_kernel(const BatchGkrInputDesc *descs, const GkrInputBlockCtx *block_ctxs, FpExt *intermediates_base, const FpExt *d_challenges)`","+"," Per-block behavior:","-- Binary search `descs[].block_start` to find this block's AIR descriptor","-- Compute `local_block_id = blockIdx.x - desc.block_start`","-- Compute row range: `row_base = local_block_id * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x`","-- Each thread processes exactly its assigned rows within `[0, desc.permutation_height)`","-- Intermediates are at `intermediates_base + desc.intermediates_offset + thread_global_id * buffer_size` — but since each block handles one AIR and intermediates are per-thread scratch, we can use `intermediates_base + air_intermediates_offset + (local_block_id * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x)`","-- The DAG evaluation code is identical to the existing kernel (same `evaluate_dag_entry_gkr`, same rule iteration, same output write)","+- Load `ctx = block_ctxs[blockIdx.x]`","+- Load `desc = descs[ctx.air_idx]`","+- Compute `row = ctx.local_block_idx * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x`","+- Guard: `if (row >= desc.permutation_height) return`","+- Compute intermediates pointer: `FpExt *my_intermediates = intermediates_base + desc.intermediates_offset + row * desc.buffer_size`","+- Set `intermediate_stride = 1` (contiguous per-thread scratch)","+- Execute the same DAG evaluation loop as `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel` (lines 98-206 of current kernel)","+- Write output to `desc.d_fracs` using `desc.permutation_height` for indexing"," ","-**Intermediates layout**: For each AIR, allocate `min(height, blocks_per_air * 256) * buffer_size` entries. Each thread's intermediates start at `air_offset + thread_local_id * buffer_size` with stride 1 (NOT `task_stride`). This means `intermediate_stride = 1` in the DAG evaluation, which removes the strided access pattern and improves cache locality.","+**Intermediates layout**: Each batch-eligible AIR allocates `height * buffer_size` entries in the shared intermediates buffer. Thread at row `r` uses entries `[intermediates_offset + r * buffer_size, intermediates_offset + (r+1) * buffer_size)`. Since different AIRs have different `buffer_size` values, the per-AIR allocation varies. The Rust side computes `intermediates_offset` via prefix sum over `height * buffer_size` for each eligible AIR."," ","-Wait — the current GLOBAL kernel uses `intermediate_stride = task_stride` (65536). The intermediates for node `i` are at `intermediates_ptr + i * 65536`. This is a huge stride. With the batched kernel, using `intermediate_stride = 1` (per-thread contiguous) would be a locality improvement. But the DAG evaluation reads `intermediates_ptr[z_index * intermediate_stride]`. With stride=1, consecutive DAG nodes are contiguous in each thread's scratch space.","+**Why stride=1 is correct for batch-eligible AIRs**: These AIRs have `num_rows_per_tile == 1`, meaning each thread processes exactly ONE row and exits. The intermediates are used only within that single row evaluation — no reuse across tile iterations. With stride=1, consecutive DAG node results for the same thread are contiguous, improving L1 cache locality vs the current stride=65536 layout."," ","-**Design**: Use per-thread contiguous intermediates (stride=1, like SCATTER mode but in global memory). Each thread's scratch region is `buffer_size` elements. The total intermediates per AIR is `num_threads_per_air * buffer_size`.","+Add a C launcher: `_batched_gkr_input_eval(const BatchGkrInputDesc *descs, const GkrInputBlockCtx *block_ctxs, uint32_t total_blocks, FpExt *intermediates, const FpExt *d_challenges)`."," ","-Add a C launcher: `_batched_gkr_input_eval(const BatchGkrInputDesc *descs, uint32_t num_descs, FpExt *intermediates, const FpExt *d_challenges)`.","-"," ### 2. Rust FFI bindings (`crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`)"," "," Add:","-- `#[repr(C)] struct BatchGkrInputDesc` matching the CUDA struct","-- `unsafe impl Send for BatchGkrInputDesc`","-- `unsafe impl Sync for BatchGkrInputDesc`","-- `extern \"C\" fn _batched_gkr_input_eval(...)` declaration","-- Safe wrapper `batched_gkr_input_eval_gpu(descs: &DeviceBuffer<BatchGkrInputDesc>, intermediates: &DeviceBuffer<EF>, d_challenges: &DeviceBuffer<EF>)`","+- `#[repr(C)] struct BatchGkrInputDesc` matching the CUDA struct (with raw pointer fields)","+- `#[repr(C)] struct GkrInputBlockCtx { local_block_idx: u32, air_idx: u32 }`","+- `unsafe impl Send for BatchGkrInputDesc` + `Sync`","+- `unsafe impl Send for GkrInputBlockCtx` + `Sync`","+- `extern \"C\" fn _batched_gkr_input_eval(descs: *const BatchGkrInputDesc, block_ctxs: *const GkrInputBlockCtx, total_blocks: u32, intermediates: *mut EF, d_challenges: *const EF) -> i32`","+- Safe wrapper `batched_gkr_input_eval_gpu(d_descs: &DeviceBuffer<BatchGkrInputDesc>, d_block_ctxs: &DeviceBuffer<GkrInputBlockCtx>, intermediates: &mut DeviceBuffer<EF>, d_challenges: &DeviceBuffer<EF>)`"," "," ### 3. Rust orchestration (`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`)"," "," Modify `log_gkr_input_evals()`:"," "," **Phase 1 (extended)**: When building work items, also collect per-AIR metadata needed for the batched path:","-- `is_global` flag","-- `buffer_size`","-- For GLOBAL AIRs with `height ≤ TASK_SIZE` and `num_rows_per_tile == 1`: mark as `batch_eligible`","+- `is_global` flag (from `buffer_size > 10`)","+- `buffer_size` value","+- `height`","+- For GLOBAL AIRs with `height ≤ TASK_SIZE` (i.e., `num_rows_per_tile == 1`): mark as `batch_eligible`"," ","-**New Phase 1.5**: Build batched descriptors for eligible AIRs:","-- Compute `blocks_per_air = height.div_ceil(256)` for each eligible AIR","-- Assign `block_start` by prefix sum","-- Compute `intermediates_offset` by prefix sum of `min(height, blocks_per_air * 256) * buffer_size`","-- Build `Vec<BatchGkrInputDesc>` on CPU","-- Upload descriptors to device: `descs.to_device()`","-- Allocate shared intermediates buffer: `DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(total_intermediates)`","+**New Phase 1.5 — Build batched descriptors for eligible AIRs**:","+1. Partition work items into `batch_eligible` and `non_eligible` lists","+2. For each batch-eligible AIR:","+   - Compute `blocks_per_air = height.div_ceil(256)` (matches kernel block size of 256)","+   - Compute `intermediates_per_air = height * buffer_size` (in EF elements)","+3. Prefix-sum `block_start` values: `block_start[i] = sum(blocks_per_air[0..i])`","+4. Prefix-sum `intermediates_offset` values: `offset[i] = sum(intermediates_per_air[0..i])`","+5. Build `Vec<BatchGkrInputDesc>` with all per-AIR pointers and metadata","+6. Build `Vec<GkrInputBlockCtx>` by iterating: for each AIR `a`, for `b` in `0..blocks_per_air[a]`, push `GkrInputBlockCtx { local_block_idx: b, air_idx: a }`","+7. Collect per-AIR `partition_ptrs` into a flat `Vec<u64>`, upload once via `to_device()`. Each descriptor's `d_main` points into this buffer.","+8. Collect per-AIR `public_values` into a flat `Vec<F>`, upload once. Each descriptor's `d_public_values` points into this buffer (or null if empty).","+9. Upload `descs.to_device()`, `block_ctxs.to_device()`","+10. Allocate shared intermediates: `DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(total_intermediates)`"," ","-**Phase 3 (modified)**: Split worker processing:","-- **Batched path** (main thread, before spawning workers): Launch one batched kernel for all eligible AIRs. No per-AIR kernel launches.","-- **Multi-stream path** (worker threads): Process remaining non-eligible AIRs (large AIRs with `height > TASK_SIZE` and all SCATTER AIRs) using the existing per-AIR `process_gkr_input_air()` logic.","+**Phase 3 (modified)**: Run batched and multi-stream paths concurrently:","+- Launch the batched kernel on a **dedicated background stream** (not the main thread's stream) so it runs concurrently with the multi-stream workers","+- Spawn worker threads for non-eligible AIRs (large GLOBAL + all SCATTER) using existing `process_gkr_input_air()` logic","+- After all workers join, sync the background stream to ensure batched kernel completed","+- Then run height-normalization (`frac_vector_scalar_multiply_ext_fp` + `frac_matrix_vertically_repeat`) for batch-eligible AIRs that need it (where `height != lifted_height`)"," ","-**Threshold**: AIRs are batch-eligible if `is_global && height <= TASK_SIZE` (i.e., `num_rows_per_tile == 1`). This covers most GLOBAL AIRs at APC 300 (height ≤ 65536).","+**APC 0 behavior**: At APC 0, `num_threads = 1` (< 100 AIRs). The batched kernel still runs for eligible GLOBAL AIRs, replacing sequential per-AIR launches. Non-eligible AIRs run on the main thread sequentially. This should be at least as fast."," ","-**Concurrency**: Launch the batched kernel on the main thread's stream, then spawn worker threads for non-eligible AIRs. The batched kernel and worker kernels run concurrently on different streams.","+### 4. Handle per-AIR data on device"," ","-### 4. Handle per-AIR data uploads","+- **Rules, used_nodes, pair_idxs**: Already on device as `pk_air.other_data.interaction_rules.inner.d_rules` etc. (uploaded at keygen time). Descriptors store raw pointers to these existing device buffers.","+- **partition_ptrs**: Currently uploaded per-AIR via `partition_ptrs.copy_to(&mut buffers.partition_ptrs)`. For the batched path, collect all partition pointer lists into one flat device buffer, with each descriptor pointing to its section.","+- **public_values**: Same pattern — flat buffer with per-AIR offsets."," ","-Each eligible AIR needs its rules, used_nodes, and pair_idxs already on the device. These are stored in `pk_air.other_data.interaction_rules.inner.d_rules` etc. — they're already device buffers uploaded at keygen time. The descriptor just stores pointers to these existing device buffers.","-","-For `partition_ptrs` (main trace partition pointers): currently uploaded per-AIR in `process_gkr_input_air`. For the batched path, collect all partition pointers into a single flat device buffer, with the descriptor pointing to each AIR's section.","-","-For `public_values`: similarly collect into a flat buffer or skip (many AIRs have empty public values).","-"," ## Invariants"," "," 1. **Output correctness**: Each row in `d_fracs` must produce the same value as the per-AIR kernel. The DAG evaluation logic is identical; only the launch geometry and intermediates layout change."," 2. **No cross-AIR interference**: Each AIR's blocks write to disjoint regions of `d_fracs` (guaranteed by different `leaves_ptr` offsets from the stacked layout).","-3. **Intermediates isolation**: Each thread's intermediates scratch is at a unique offset (guaranteed by the prefix-sum allocation).","-4. **Fallback for large AIRs**: AIRs with `height > TASK_SIZE` (`num_rows_per_tile > 1`) continue using the existing per-AIR kernel. The tile loop requires intermediates at `task_stride` width, which doesn't fit the batched layout.","-5. **APC 0 regression**: At APC 0 (99 AIRs, below multi-stream threshold), all processing is single-threaded. The batched kernel replaces sequential per-AIR launches with one batched launch, which should be at least as fast.","-6. **Height < lifted_height normalization**: AIRs where `height != lifted_height` need post-processing (`frac_vector_scalar_multiply_ext_fp` + `frac_matrix_vertically_repeat`). These must run AFTER the batched kernel completes. Add a `current_stream_sync()` after the batched launch, then run normalization for each eligible AIR that needs it.","+3. **Intermediates isolation**: Each thread's scratch is at a unique offset: `intermediates_offset + row * buffer_size` for `row < permutation_height`. No two threads share scratch space. Different AIRs have different `intermediates_offset` values (prefix sum ensures non-overlap). Different rows within an AIR have different `row` values.","+4. **Varying buffer_size**: The `buffer_size` field in each descriptor tells the kernel how many EF elements to stride per row. The intermediates offset computation on the Rust side uses each AIR's own `buffer_size` to compute the prefix sum correctly.","+5. **Fallback for large AIRs**: AIRs with `height > TASK_SIZE` (`num_rows_per_tile > 1`) continue using the existing per-AIR kernel on worker threads. The tile loop in those kernels reuses intermediates across tiles (same thread processes multiple rows), requiring the current `task_stride` layout. The batched kernel's stride=1 layout only works when `num_rows_per_tile == 1`.","+6. **APC 0 safety**: At APC 0 (99 AIRs, below multi-stream threshold), the batched kernel replaces sequential per-AIR launches. Non-eligible AIRs run sequentially. No regression expected.","+7. **Height normalization**: Eligible AIRs where `height != lifted_height` need post-processing after the batched kernel. Sync the background stream first, then run normalization kernels on the main stream."," "," ## Measurement Plan"," "]},{"oldStart":142,"oldLines":8,"newStart":159,"newLines":8,"lines":[" 3. Benchmark APC 0: `cd /home/georg/powdr && openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh` (APC 0 only)"," 4. Benchmark APC 300: same script, APC 300"," 5. Compare:","-   - LogUp GKR at APC 300: expect 529ms → ~450-480ms","-   - STARK excl trace at APC 300: expect 1301ms → ~1240-1260ms","+   - LogUp GKR at APC 300: expect 529ms → ~490-510ms","+   - STARK excl trace at APC 300: expect 1301ms → ~1260-1280ms","    - No regression at APC 0"," "," ## Rollback Criteria"]}],"originalFile":"# Plan: Batched Descriptor-Array GLOBAL-Mode GKR Input Evaluation\n\n## Goal\n\nReplace per-AIR GLOBAL-mode GKR input evaluation kernel launches with a single batched descriptor-array kernel for small AIRs (height ≤ TASK_SIZE). This targets the largest STARK excl trace component: LogUp GKR at 529ms (41% of 1301ms total). The GKR input eval accounts for ~310ms of wall-clock time with 509ms of GPU kernel time compressed to 310ms via 8-stream multi-threading (1.64x compression). By using GPU-native block scheduling instead of coarse-grained stream scheduling, we expect to improve compression to ~2.0-2.5x, saving 40-80ms.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### Entry point\n`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs:215` — `log_gkr_input_evals()`\n\n### Flow\n1. **Phase 1** (lines 232-252): Build work items sorted by descending height\n2. **Phase 2** (lines 254-279): Pre-compute max buffer sizes for pre-allocation\n3. **Barrier** (line 282): `current_stream_sync()` to ensure `fill_zero` is visible to workers\n4. **Phase 3** (lines 285-289): Decide thread count (≥100 AIRs → 8 threads, else 1)\n5. **Worker loop**: Each thread processes its assigned work items sequentially via `process_gkr_input_air()` (line 112)\n6. **Per-AIR processing** (line 112-209): For each AIR:\n   - Collect trace pointers, public values, interaction rules\n   - Determine GLOBAL vs SCATTER mode (`buffer_size > 10`)\n   - Launch `_logup_gkr_input_eval` CUDA kernel with `count = TASK_SIZE = 65536` for GLOBAL mode\n\n### CUDA kernel\n`crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.cu:66` — `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<GLOBAL>`\n\nFor GLOBAL mode:\n- **Grid**: 256 blocks × 256 threads = 65536 threads (always, regardless of AIR height)\n- **Intermediates**: global memory at stride `task_stride = gridDim.x * blockDim.x`\n- **Tile loop**: each thread processes `num_rows_per_tile` rows: `row = task_offset + j * task_stride`\n- **Output**: writes to `d_fracs[interaction_idx * permutation_height + row]` — per-row, no cross-thread reduction\n\n### Why it's slow\nAt APC 300 (2 segments, 623 AIR instances):\n- ~300 GLOBAL-mode AIRs per segment\n- Each launches a 256-block kernel (65536 threads)\n- Small AIRs (height 256-4096) use <6% of launched threads; the rest check `row < permutation_height` and exit\n- 8-stream multi-threading achieves only 1.64x compression (509ms → 310ms) because:\n  - Each 256-block launch briefly occupies all 128 SMs (2 waves), blocking other streams\n  - The GPU scheduler can only interleave kernels between launches, not within a launch\n  - Per-launch overhead (kernel launch API ~5μs × 300 launches = ~1.5ms per segment) adds up\n\n### Key property enabling batching\n**Output independence**: Each row's output goes to a unique position in `d_fracs`. There is NO cross-row or cross-thread reduction. Intermediates are per-thread scratch (rewritten for each row in the tile loop). This means different AIRs' computations are fully independent and can share a kernel launch.\n\n## Changes\n\n### 1. CUDA batched kernel (`crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.cu`)\n\nAdd a descriptor struct and batched kernel:\n\n```c\nstruct BatchGkrInputDesc {\n    FracExt *d_fracs;          // output base for this AIR\n    const Fp *d_preprocessed;  // preprocessed trace\n    const uint64_t *d_main;    // partition pointers\n    const Fp *d_public_values; // public values\n    const Rule *d_rules;       // interaction rules\n    const size_t *d_used_nodes;\n    const uint32_t *d_pair_idxs;\n    size_t used_nodes_len;\n    uint32_t permutation_height;\n    uint32_t block_start;      // first block for this AIR\n    uint32_t block_end;        // exclusive end block for this AIR\n};\n```\n\nAdd a `BlockCtx` that maps `blockIdx.x → (air_desc_index, local_block_id)` via binary search on `block_start` (same pattern as `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.cu`).\n\nNew kernel: `batched_gkr_input_eval_kernel(const BatchGkrInputDesc *descs, uint32_t num_descs, FpExt *intermediates_base, const FpExt *d_challenges)`\n\nPer-block behavior:\n- Binary search `descs[].block_start` to find this block's AIR descriptor\n- Compute `local_block_id = blockIdx.x - desc.block_start`\n- Compute row range: `row_base = local_block_id * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x`\n- Each thread processes exactly its assigned rows within `[0, desc.permutation_height)`\n- Intermediates are at `intermediates_base + desc.intermediates_offset + thread_global_id * buffer_size` — but since each block handles one AIR and intermediates are per-thread scratch, we can use `intermediates_base + air_intermediates_offset + (local_block_id * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x)`\n- The DAG evaluation code is identical to the existing kernel (same `evaluate_dag_entry_gkr`, same rule iteration, same output write)\n\n**Intermediates layout**: For each AIR, allocate `min(height, blocks_per_air * 256) * buffer_size` entries. Each thread's intermediates start at `air_offset + thread_local_id * buffer_size` with stride 1 (NOT `task_stride`). This means `intermediate_stride = 1` in the DAG evaluation, which removes the strided access pattern and improves cache locality.\n\nWait — the current GLOBAL kernel uses `intermediate_stride = task_stride` (65536). The intermediates for node `i` are at `intermediates_ptr + i * 65536`. This is a huge stride. With the batched kernel, using `intermediate_stride = 1` (per-thread contiguous) would be a locality improvement. But the DAG evaluation reads `intermediates_ptr[z_index * intermediate_stride]`. With stride=1, consecutive DAG nodes are contiguous in each thread's scratch space.\n\n**Design**: Use per-thread contiguous intermediates (stride=1, like SCATTER mode but in global memory). Each thread's scratch region is `buffer_size` elements. The total intermediates per AIR is `num_threads_per_air * buffer_size`.\n\nAdd a C launcher: `_batched_gkr_input_eval(const BatchGkrInputDesc *descs, uint32_t num_descs, FpExt *intermediates, const FpExt *d_challenges)`.\n\n### 2. Rust FFI bindings (`crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`)\n\nAdd:\n- `#[repr(C)] struct BatchGkrInputDesc` matching the CUDA struct\n- `unsafe impl Send for BatchGkrInputDesc`\n- `unsafe impl Sync for BatchGkrInputDesc`\n- `extern \"C\" fn _batched_gkr_input_eval(...)` declaration\n- Safe wrapper `batched_gkr_input_eval_gpu(descs: &DeviceBuffer<BatchGkrInputDesc>, intermediates: &DeviceBuffer<EF>, d_challenges: &DeviceBuffer<EF>)`\n\n### 3. Rust orchestration (`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`)\n\nModify `log_gkr_input_evals()`:\n\n**Phase 1 (extended)**: When building work items, also collect per-AIR metadata needed for the batched path:\n- `is_global` flag\n- `buffer_size`\n- For GLOBAL AIRs with `height ≤ TASK_SIZE` and `num_rows_per_tile == 1`: mark as `batch_eligible`\n\n**New Phase 1.5**: Build batched descriptors for eligible AIRs:\n- Compute `blocks_per_air = height.div_ceil(256)` for each eligible AIR\n- Assign `block_start` by prefix sum\n- Compute `intermediates_offset` by prefix sum of `min(height, blocks_per_air * 256) * buffer_size`\n- Build `Vec<BatchGkrInputDesc>` on CPU\n- Upload descriptors to device: `descs.to_device()`\n- Allocate shared intermediates buffer: `DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(total_intermediates)`\n\n**Phase 3 (modified)**: Split worker processing:\n- **Batched path** (main thread, before spawning workers): Launch one batched kernel for all eligible AIRs. No per-AIR kernel launches.\n- **Multi-stream path** (worker threads): Process remaining non-eligible AIRs (large AIRs with `height > TASK_SIZE` and all SCATTER AIRs) using the existing per-AIR `process_gkr_input_air()` logic.\n\n**Threshold**: AIRs are batch-eligible if `is_global && height <= TASK_SIZE` (i.e., `num_rows_per_tile == 1`). This covers most GLOBAL AIRs at APC 300 (height ≤ 65536).\n\n**Concurrency**: Launch the batched kernel on the main thread's stream, then spawn worker threads for non-eligible AIRs. The batched kernel and worker kernels run concurrently on different streams.\n\n### 4. Handle per-AIR data uploads\n\nEach eligible AIR needs its rules, used_nodes, and pair_idxs already on the device. These are stored in `pk_air.other_data.interaction_rules.inner.d_rules` etc. — they're already device buffers uploaded at keygen time. The descriptor just stores pointers to these existing device buffers.\n\nFor `partition_ptrs` (main trace partition pointers): currently uploaded per-AIR in `process_gkr_input_air`. For the batched path, collect all partition pointers into a single flat device buffer, with the descriptor pointing to each AIR's section.\n\nFor `public_values`: similarly collect into a flat buffer or skip (many AIRs have empty public values).\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Output correctness**: Each row in `d_fracs` must produce the same value as the per-AIR kernel. The DAG evaluation logic is identical; only the launch geometry and intermediates layout change.\n2. **No cross-AIR interference**: Each AIR's blocks write to disjoint regions of `d_fracs` (guaranteed by different `leaves_ptr` offsets from the stacked layout).\n3. **Intermediates isolation**: Each thread's intermediates scratch is at a unique offset (guaranteed by the prefix-sum allocation).\n4. **Fallback for large AIRs**: AIRs with `height > TASK_SIZE` (`num_rows_per_tile > 1`) continue using the existing per-AIR kernel. The tile loop requires intermediates at `task_stride` width, which doesn't fit the batched layout.\n5. **APC 0 regression**: At APC 0 (99 AIRs, below multi-stream threshold), all processing is single-threaded. The batched kernel replaces sequential per-AIR launches with one batched launch, which should be at least as fast.\n6. **Height < lifted_height normalization**: AIRs where `height != lifted_height` need post-processing (`frac_vector_scalar_multiply_ext_fp` + `frac_matrix_vertically_repeat`). These must run AFTER the batched kernel completes. Add a `current_stream_sync()` after the batched launch, then run normalization for each eligible AIR that needs it.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n1. Build: `cargo check -p openvm-cuda-backend`\n2. Test: `cargo nextest run -p openvm-cuda-backend --test-threads=4`\n3. Benchmark APC 0: `cd /home/georg/powdr && openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh` (APC 0 only)\n4. Benchmark APC 300: same script, APC 300\n5. Compare:\n   - LogUp GKR at APC 300: expect 529ms → ~450-480ms\n   - STARK excl trace at APC 300: expect 1301ms → ~1240-1260ms\n   - No regression at APC 0\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\n- Less than 20ms improvement on STARK excl trace at APC 300\n- Any regression at APC 0 (STARK excl trace increase > 15ms)\n- Test failures\n- cudaErrorIllegalAddress or other CUDA errors\n"}}
