{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01L2Yo6f7tp1Nn2CMVQgzZma","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Batched Descriptor-Array GLOBAL-Mode GKR Input Evaluation\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tReplace per-AIR GLOBAL-mode GKR input evaluation kernel launches with a single batched descriptor-array kernel for small AIRs (height ≤ TASK_SIZE). This targets the largest STARK excl trace component: LogUp GKR at 529ms (41% of 1301ms total). The GKR input eval accounts for ~310ms of wall-clock time with 509ms of GPU kernel time compressed to 310ms via 8-stream multi-threading (1.64x compression). By using GPU-native block scheduling instead of coarse-grained stream scheduling and eliminating ~300 full-grid (256-block) kernel launches per segment, we expect to save 20-50ms from LogUp GKR.\n6\t\n7\t**Calibration against prior work**: Three prior optimizations targeted this same phase — right-sizing grid (0ms), intermediates locality change (0ms), and SCATTER batching (-8ms). The key difference here is that GLOBAL AIRs each launch 256 blocks (vs 4-16 for SCATTER), and there are ~300 such launches per segment, each briefly monopolizing all 128 SMs for 2 waves. This is a fundamentally different overhead profile than the SCATTER case.\n8\t\n9\t## Current Code Path\n10\t\n11\t### Entry point\n12\t`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs:215` — `log_gkr_input_evals()`\n13\t\n14\t### Flow\n15\t1. **Phase 1** (lines 232-252): Build work items sorted by descending height\n16\t2. **Phase 2** (lines 254-279): Pre-compute max buffer sizes for pre-allocation\n17\t3. **Barrier** (line 282): `current_stream_sync()` to ensure `fill_zero` is visible to workers\n18\t4. **Phase 3** (lines 285-289): Decide thread count (≥100 AIRs → 8 threads, else 1)\n19\t5. **Worker loop**: Each thread processes its assigned work items sequentially via `process_gkr_input_air()` (line 112)\n20\t6. **Per-AIR processing** (line 112-209): For each AIR:\n21\t   - Collect trace pointers, public values, interaction rules\n22\t   - Determine GLOBAL vs SCATTER mode (`buffer_size > 10`, line 160)\n23\t   - Launch `_logup_gkr_input_eval` CUDA kernel with `count = TASK_SIZE = 65536` for GLOBAL mode\n24\t\n25\t### CUDA kernel\n26\t`crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.cu:66` — `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<GLOBAL>`\n27\t\n28\tFor GLOBAL mode:\n29\t- **Grid**: 256 blocks × 256 threads = 65536 threads (always, regardless of AIR height)\n30\t- **Intermediates**: global memory, `intermediates_ptr = d_intermediates + task_offset`, `intermediate_stride = task_stride = gridDim.x * blockDim.x`\n31\t- **Tile loop**: each thread processes `num_rows_per_tile` rows: `row = task_offset + j * task_stride`\n32\t- **Output**: writes to `d_fracs[(interaction_idx * permutation_height + row) * 2 + is_denom]` — per-row, no cross-thread reduction\n33\t\n34\t### Why it's slow\n35\tAt APC 300 (2 segments, 623 AIR instances):\n36\t- ~300 GLOBAL-mode AIRs per segment (those with `buffer_size > 10`)\n37\t- Each launches a 256-block kernel (65536 threads)\n38\t- Small AIRs (height 256-4096) use <6% of launched threads; the rest check `row < permutation_height` and exit\n39\t- 8-stream multi-threading achieves only 1.64x compression (509ms GPU → 310ms wall) because:\n40\t  - Each 256-block launch briefly occupies all 128 SMs (2 waves), blocking other streams' kernels\n41\t  - Per-launch overhead (~5μs × 300 launches = ~1.5ms per segment) and CPU-side per-AIR setup (H2D for partition_ptrs, public_values) add up\n42\t  - CPU iteration to collect per-AIR metadata before each launch\n43\t\n44\t### Key property enabling batching\n45\t**Output independence**: Each row's output goes to a unique position in `d_fracs`. There is NO cross-row or cross-thread reduction. Intermediates are per-thread scratch (rewritten for each row in the tile loop, since `num_rows_per_tile == 1` for batch-eligible AIRs). This means different AIRs' computations are fully independent and can share a kernel launch.\n46\t\n47\t## Changes\n48\t\n49\t### 1. CUDA batched kernel (`crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.cu`)\n50\t\n51\tAdd a descriptor struct:\n52\t\n53\t```c\n54\tstruct BatchGkrInputDesc {\n55\t    FracExt *d_fracs;           // output base for this AIR (pre-offset by stacked layout)\n56\t    const Fp *d_preprocessed;   // preprocessed trace buffer\n57\t    const uint64_t *d_main;     // partition pointers (device)\n58\t    const Fp *d_public_values;  // public values (device, may be null)\n59\t    const Rule *d_rules;        // interaction rules\n60\t    const size_t *d_used_nodes; // used nodes array\n61\t    const uint32_t *d_pair_idxs;// pair index array\n62\t    size_t used_nodes_len;      // length of used_nodes\n63\t    uint32_t permutation_height;// this AIR's height\n64\t    uint32_t buffer_size;       // intermediates scratch size per thread for this AIR\n65\t    uint32_t intermediates_offset; // offset into shared intermediates buffer (in EF elements)\n66\t    uint32_t block_start;       // first block index for this AIR (for BlockCtx lookup)\n67\t};\n68\t```\n69\t\n70\tAdd a `BlockCtx` array (one entry per block, pre-built on Rust side) following the pattern in `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.cu:119`:\n71\t\n72\t```c\n73\tstruct GkrInputBlockCtx {\n74\t    uint32_t local_block_idx;  // block index within this AIR\n75\t    uint32_t air_idx;          // index into BatchGkrInputDesc array\n76\t};\n77\t```\n78\t\n79\tNew kernel: `batched_gkr_input_eval_kernel(const BatchGkrInputDesc *descs, const GkrInputBlockCtx *block_ctxs, FpExt *intermediates_base, const FpExt *d_challenges)`\n80\t\n81\tPer-block behavior:\n82\t- Load `ctx = block_ctxs[blockIdx.x]`\n83\t- Load `desc = descs[ctx.air_idx]`\n84\t- Compute `row = ctx.local_block_idx * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x`\n85\t- Guard: `if (row >= desc.permutation_height) return`\n86\t- Compute intermediates pointer: `FpExt *my_intermediates = intermediates_base + desc.intermediates_offset + row * desc.buffer_size`\n87\t- Set `intermediate_stride = 1` (contiguous per-thread scratch)\n88\t- Execute the same DAG evaluation loop as `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel` (lines 98-206 of current kernel)\n89\t- Write output to `desc.d_fracs` using `desc.permutation_height` for indexing\n90\t\n91\t**Intermediates layout**: Each batch-eligible AIR allocates `height * buffer_size` entries in the shared intermediates buffer. Thread at row `r` uses entries `[intermediates_offset + r * buffer_size, intermediates_offset + (r+1) * buffer_size)`. Since different AIRs have different `buffer_size` values, the per-AIR allocation varies. The Rust side computes `intermediates_offset` via prefix sum over `height * buffer_size` for each eligible AIR.\n92\t\n93\t**Why stride=1 is correct for batch-eligible AIRs**: These AIRs have `num_rows_per_tile == 1`, meaning each thread processes exactly ONE row and exits. The intermediates are used only within that single row evaluation — no reuse across tile iterations. With stride=1, consecutive DAG node results for the same thread are contiguous, improving L1 cache locality vs the current stride=65536 layout.\n94\t\n95\tAdd a C launcher: `_batched_gkr_input_eval(const BatchGkrInputDesc *descs, const GkrInputBlockCtx *block_ctxs, uint32_t total_blocks, FpExt *intermediates, const FpExt *d_challenges)`.\n96\t\n97\t### 2. Rust FFI bindings (`crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`)\n98\t\n99\tAdd:\n100\t- `#[repr(C)] struct BatchGkrInputDesc` matching the CUDA struct (with raw pointer fields)\n101\t- `#[repr(C)] struct GkrInputBlockCtx { local_block_idx: u32, air_idx: u32 }`\n102\t- `unsafe impl Send for BatchGkrInputDesc` + `Sync`\n103\t- `unsafe impl Send for GkrInputBlockCtx` + `Sync`\n104\t- `extern \"C\" fn _batched_gkr_input_eval(descs: *const BatchGkrInputDesc, block_ctxs: *const GkrInputBlockCtx, total_blocks: u32, intermediates: *mut EF, d_challenges: *const EF) -> i32`\n105\t- Safe wrapper `batched_gkr_input_eval_gpu(d_descs: &DeviceBuffer<BatchGkrInputDesc>, d_block_ctxs: &DeviceBuffer<GkrInputBlockCtx>, intermediates: &mut DeviceBuffer<EF>, d_challenges: &DeviceBuffer<EF>)`\n106\t\n107\t### 3. Rust orchestration (`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`)\n108\t\n109\tModify `log_gkr_input_evals()`:\n110\t\n111\t**Phase 1 (extended)**: When building work items, also collect per-AIR metadata needed for the batched path:\n112\t- `is_global` flag (from `buffer_size > 10`)\n113\t- `buffer_size` value\n114\t- `height`\n115\t- For GLOBAL AIRs with `height ≤ TASK_SIZE` (i.e., `num_rows_per_tile == 1`): mark as `batch_eligible`\n116\t\n117\t**New Phase 1.5 — Build batched descriptors for eligible AIRs**:\n118\t1. Partition work items into `batch_eligible` and `non_eligible` lists\n119\t2. For each batch-eligible AIR:\n120\t   - Compute `blocks_per_air = height.div_ceil(256)` (matches kernel block size of 256)\n121\t   - Compute `intermediates_per_air = height * buffer_size` (in EF elements)\n122\t3. Prefix-sum `block_start` values: `block_start[i] = sum(blocks_per_air[0..i])`\n123\t4. Prefix-sum `intermediates_offset` values: `offset[i] = sum(intermediates_per_air[0..i])`\n124\t5. Build `Vec<BatchGkrInputDesc>` with all per-AIR pointers and metadata\n125\t6. Build `Vec<GkrInputBlockCtx>` by iterating: for each AIR `a`, for `b` in `0..blocks_per_air[a]`, push `GkrInputBlockCtx { local_block_idx: b, air_idx: a }`\n126\t7. Collect per-AIR `partition_ptrs` into a flat `Vec<u64>`, upload once via `to_device()`. Each descriptor's `d_main` points into this buffer.\n127\t8. Collect per-AIR `public_values` into a flat `Vec<F>`, upload once. Each descriptor's `d_public_values` points into this buffer (or null if empty).\n128\t9. Upload `descs.to_device()`, `block_ctxs.to_device()`\n129\t10. Allocate shared intermediates: `DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(total_intermediates)`\n130\t\n131\t**Phase 3 (modified)**: Run batched and multi-stream paths concurrently:\n132\t- Launch the batched kernel on a **dedicated background stream** (not the main thread's stream) so it runs concurrently with the multi-stream workers\n133\t- Spawn worker threads for non-eligible AIRs (large GLOBAL + all SCATTER) using existing `process_gkr_input_air()` logic\n134\t- After all workers join, sync the background stream to ensure batched kernel completed\n135\t- Then run height-normalization (`frac_vector_scalar_multiply_ext_fp` + `frac_matrix_vertically_repeat`) for batch-eligible AIRs that need it (where `height != lifted_height`)\n136\t\n137\t**APC 0 behavior**: At APC 0, `num_threads = 1` (< 100 AIRs). The batched kernel still runs for eligible GLOBAL AIRs, replacing sequential per-AIR launches. Non-eligible AIRs run on the main thread sequentially. This should be at least as fast.\n138\t\n139\t### 4. Handle per-AIR data on device\n140\t\n141\t- **Rules, used_nodes, pair_idxs**: Already on device as `pk_air.other_data.interaction_rules.inner.d_rules` etc. (uploaded at keygen time). Descriptors store raw pointers to these existing device buffers.\n142\t- **partition_ptrs**: Currently uploaded per-AIR via `partition_ptrs.copy_to(&mut buffers.partition_ptrs)`. For the batched path, collect all partition pointer lists into one flat device buffer, with each descriptor pointing to its section.\n143\t- **public_values**: Same pattern — flat buffer with per-AIR offsets.\n144\t\n145\t## Invariants\n146\t\n147\t1. **Output correctness**: Each row in `d_fracs` must produce the same value as the per-AIR kernel. The DAG evaluation logic is identical; only the launch geometry and intermediates layout change.\n148\t2. **No cross-AIR interference**: Each AIR's blocks write to disjoint regions of `d_fracs` (guaranteed by different `leaves_ptr` offsets from the stacked layout).\n149\t3. **Intermediates isolation**: Each thread's scratch is at a unique offset: `intermediates_offset + row * buffer_size` for `row < permutation_height`. No two threads share scratch space. Different AIRs have different `intermediates_offset` values (prefix sum ensures non-overlap). Different rows within an AIR have different `row` values.\n150\t4. **Varying buffer_size**: The `buffer_size` field in each descriptor tells the kernel how many EF elements to stride per row. The intermediates offset computation on the Rust side uses each AIR's own `buffer_size` to compute the prefix sum correctly.\n151\t5. **Fallback for large AIRs**: AIRs with `height > TASK_SIZE` (`num_rows_per_tile > 1`) continue using the existing per-AIR kernel on worker threads. The tile loop in those kernels reuses intermediates across tiles (same thread processes multiple rows), requiring the current `task_stride` layout. The batched kernel's stride=1 layout only works when `num_rows_per_tile == 1`.\n152\t6. **APC 0 safety**: At APC 0 (99 AIRs, below multi-stream threshold), the batched kernel replaces sequential per-AIR launches. Non-eligible AIRs run sequentially. No regression expected.\n153\t7. **Height normalization**: Eligible AIRs where `height != lifted_height` need post-processing after the batched kernel. Sync the background stream first, then run normalization kernels on the main stream.\n154\t\n155\t## Measurement Plan\n156\t\n157\t1. Build: `cargo check -p openvm-cuda-backend`\n158\t2. Test: `cargo nextest run -p openvm-cuda-backend --test-threads=4`\n159\t3. Benchmark APC 0: `cd /home/georg/powdr && openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh` (APC 0 only)\n160\t4. Benchmark APC 300: same script, APC 300\n161\t5. Compare:\n162\t   - LogUp GKR at APC 300: expect 529ms → ~490-510ms\n163\t   - STARK excl trace at APC 300: expect 1301ms → ~1260-1280ms\n164\t   - No regression at APC 0\n165\t\n166\t## Rollback Criteria\n167\t\n168\t- Less than 20ms improvement on STARK excl trace at APC 300\n169\t- Any regression at APC 0 (STARK excl trace increase > 15ms)\n170\t- Test failures\n171\t- cudaErrorIllegalAddress or other CUDA errors\n172\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LAUawgWZuvpEDpDTF67JV1","session_id":"5c4196e5-21f2-4e88-ab6f-fb2d2870d4db","uuid":"71659164-f56c-46ef-8eee-e73c47e46539","timestamp":"2026-04-14T23:08:01.834Z"}
